The U.S. state of [[New York (state)|New York]] was the first to require its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s, in 1901. Registrants provided their own [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s for display, featuring their initials until 1903 and numbers thereafter, until the state began to issue plates in 1910.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-new-york-license-plates-vintage-new-york-license-plates/ |title=Old New York License Plates |date=27 August 2015 |publisher=LeatherLicensePlates.com |access-date=August 5, 2017}}</ref>

Plates are currently issued by the [[New York State Department of Motor Vehicles]] (NYSDMV). Front and rear plates are required on all vehicles except for motorcycles and trailers.{{cn|date=June 2025}}

==Passenger baseplates==
===1910 to 1965===
In 1956, the United States, Canada and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The 1956 (dated 1957) issue was the first New York license plate that complied with these standards.{{cn|date=June 2025}}
